   "item": {
    "subject_key": "ap-statistics",
    "unit_number": 3,
    "topic_code": "3.6",
    "topic_title": "p-Values",
    "stem": "A school's records suggest that 40% of its students bike to school. A club suspects the proportion is now higher. They take a random sample of 150 students and find that 71 bike, so p̂ ≈ 0.47. They test H₀: p = 0.40 against Hₐ: p > 0.40, where p is the proportion of all students at the school who bike to school. The test gives z ≈ 1.75 and a p-value of about 0.04. Which statement correctly interprets this p-value?",
    "choices": [
     {
      "choice_key": "A",
      "choice_text": "If the true proportion of all students at the school who bike is 0.40, the probability of getting a sample proportion of 0.47 or higher in a random sample of 150 students is about 0.04.",
      "is_correct": true,
      "rationale": "A p-value is computed assuming H₀ is true. It gives the probability of a sample result at least as extreme as the observed one, in the direction of Hₐ. Here that means p̂ ≥ 0.47 when p = 0.40 and n = 150. The statement is also written in the study's context."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "B",
      "choice_text": "There is about a 0.04 probability that the true proportion of all students at the school who bike is 0.40.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "This is the common error of treating the p-value as the probability that the null hypothesis is true. The p-value assumes H₀ is true and measures how unusual the sample result would be. It does not give a probability for the parameter itself. Fix: Check that your sentence starts by assuming H₀ is true and then describes the probability of the sample result, not of the hypothesis."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "C",
      "choice_text": "If the true proportion of all students at the school who bike is 0.40, the probability of getting a sample proportion of exactly 0.47 in a random sample of 150 students is about 0.04.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "This statement drops the 'at least as extreme' part. The p-value is the probability of the whole tail of results p̂ ≥ 0.47, not the probability of one exact value. With the normal model, the probability of one exact value is essentially zero. Fix: Ask whether your interpretation includes the observed result 'or more extreme' in the direction of Hₐ."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "D",
      "choice_text": "If the true proportion of all students at the school who bike is greater than 0.40, the probability of getting a sample proportion of 0.47 or higher in a random sample of 150 students is about 0.04.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "This conditions on the alternative hypothesis instead of the null. The z-statistic and p-value were computed using p₀ = 0.40, so the probability only makes sense under the assumption that p = 0.40. Fix: Check which parameter value you used to compute the test statistic, and state that value as the assumption in your interpretation."
     }
    ]
   },
