   "item": {
    "subject_key": "ap-statistics",
    "unit_number": 3,
    "topic_code": "3.6",
    "topic_title": "p-Values",
    "stem": "A city surveys a simple random sample of 100 adults from its 10,000 adult residents. Of those sampled, 60 support a proposed bus route. Let p be the proportion of all adult residents who support the route. A normal-approximation test of H₀: p = 0.50 against Hₐ: p > 0.50 gives an approximate p-value of 0.023; all conditions for the test have been verified. Which statement correctly interprets this p-value?",
    "choices": [
     {
      "choice_key": "A",
      "choice_text": "Given the sample results, the probability that exactly 50% of all adult residents support the route is approximately 0.023.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "This is the probability-of-the-null trap. The p-value describes sample results under the assumption p = 0.50; it does not give the probability that this population claim is true. Fix: Begin your interpretation with the null assumption, then identify the sample outcomes whose probability is being described."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "B",
      "choice_text": "Assuming 50% of all adult residents support the route, the probability of getting exactly 60 supporters in a random sample of 100 adults is approximately 0.023.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "This is the exact-result trap. A p-value includes the observed result and results more extreme in the direction of the alternative, rather than only the single observed count. Fix: Replace “exactly” with a range that includes the observed result and all more extreme results in the alternative’s direction."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "C",
      "choice_text": "Assuming 50% of all adult residents support the route, the probability of getting 60 or fewer supporters in a random sample of 100 adults is approximately 0.023.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "This is the wrong-tail trap. The alternative p > 0.50 makes larger counts evidence against the null, so counting downward from 60 uses the wrong direction. Fix: Check the alternative’s inequality and count outcomes from the observed result toward that direction."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "D",
      "choice_text": "Assuming 50% of all adult residents support the route, the probability of getting 60 or more supporters in a random sample of 100 adults is approximately 0.023.",
      "is_correct": true,
      "rationale": "A p-value is a probability of sample results calculated assuming the null hypothesis is true. Because the alternative is p > 0.50, results at least as extreme as the observed result are 60 or more supporters, not just exactly 60."
     }
    ]
   },
